add(94,[94],'unproved claim',[62],'448–450','recouvrements arbitrairement fins finis','The author proposes a compact-base alternative using arbitrarily fine finite covers whose finite intersections have finite compact-support cohomology.','The cover spectral sequence and compatibility with local-system trivializations are not written; closed covers additionally need excision. The ambiguous cover-condition label is not a missing theorem.')
add(95,[95,97],'unproved claim',[62],'452–465','on utilise la suite spectrale d’homologie','For an ordinary local-system Euler lemma, the source changes to homological indexing and adds a homotopy/finite-cover hypothesis. For finite CW models, a rank-b local system multiplies Euler characteristic by b.','The inserted subscript p and dual coefficient system support the homological interpretation. Constant-coefficient finiteness alone remains insufficient.')
add(96,[96],'idea of the earlier run',[60,62],'363–385;454–465','Énoncé analogue','A locally finite telescope of RP^n has rational Euler number 1, as does its contractible double cover, whereas the two-point fibre has Euler number 2. Its rank-one sign local system has Euler number zero.','This checks the earlier run’s counterexample: local finiteness gives local compactness, telescope homotopy types are RP infinity and S infinity, and rational invariants are exact. It lies outside the final conical-end class.')
add(98,[98,99],'reading question',[62],'471–493','S in E_(n-1)|C_(n-1); espaces discrets','A precise compact-Hausdorff recursive cone convention is needed for C_n and the one-point-compactification class E_infinity. Infinite discrete spaces do not meet its finite-cohomology conclusion.','The notation alternates C and E, and the margin alone says discrete spaces. Infinite degree-zero cohomology rules out treating every discrete space as a compact member.','Cone on the empty link is interpreted as a point; finite-dimensional metrizability is justified inductively only under the specified compact convention.')
add(100,[100],'withdrawn in the same text',[62],'489','ils sont triang. dans le cas cpt [deleted]','The compact dimension-three triangulation clause is cancelled.','The Pro text records this deletion; Codex K93-08 line68 fails to mark it. Without scans the disagreement remains a reading check, and the deleted clause is not an authorial settled result.')
add(101,[101],'self-flagged step',[62],'491–493','il n’est pas clair qu’ils sont triangulables','The author doubts global compact triangulability in the dimension-four discussion.','Later compact topological E8 four-manifolds are locally conical yet nontriangulable. Moise’s three-manifold theorem does not remove this distinction.')
add(102,[102],'unproved claim',[62],'497–500','comme X est loc. contractile','Compact members of the standard recursive conical class have finite total cohomology.','Inductively finite-dimensional metric cone charts and compact Hausdorffness give a compact metric locally contractible space, hence an ANR. West supplies finite CW homotopy type, a modern justification stronger than the terse source.')
add(103,[103],'unproved claim',[62,66],'500–503;618','X=hat X minus omega','Deleting the compactification point from a finite-cohomology conical compactification gives finite compact-support cohomology and chi_c(X)=chi(hat X)-1.','This is a direct closed/open exact-sequence deduction once the intended support theory is fixed.')
add(104,[104],'self-flagged step',[64],'527–531','On vérifie; vérifier','Removing the open half-cone at infinity is asserted to leave a compact conical core.','New boundary points need product-with-half-interval cone charts; the page flags but does not supply this closure argument.')
add(105,[105],'self-flagged step',[64],'533','X prime sim X','The punctured compactification deformation retracts onto the compact truncated core.','In a cone chart use r -> (1-t)r+t max(r,1/2) and the identity outside. It is fixed for r>=1/2, so the two definitions glue and avoid the removed vertex.')
add(106,[106,107],'unproved claim',[64,66],'535–546;619','chi(X)-chi_!(X)=chi(S_omega)','With an excisive compact core/cone decomposition, the ordinary-minus-compact-support Euler defect equals the Euler characteristic of the end link.','Mayer–Vietoris, contractibility of the cone and the radial retract prove the identity. The R^n and collared-manifold examples are checks by the earlier run, not extra manuscript assertions.')
add(108,[108],'reading question',[66],'580','chi_!(Y|X-Y)','The first alternative gives Y minus Y where closed/open additivity needs X minus Y.','Comparison with scan56 and X=R,Y=empty separates the alternatives mathematically; Codex has X-Y, but the ink remains unexamined.')
add(109,[109],'reading question',[66],'582','X prime, X double-prime fermés dans X','The final Mayer–Vietoris summary omits X=X prime union X double-prime.','X=S1 and both pieces a point give false equality 0=1. Scan56 explicitly includes the missing cover condition.')
add(110,[110],'unpursued implication',[66],'586–588','X sim X prime; X simeq X prime','The two displayed invariances must distinguish ordinary homotopy invariance from compact-support invariance under a stronger equivalence.','R is contractible but has compact-support Euler number -1 instead of the point’s 1. Thus compact-support invariance under arbitrary homotopy is not available.')
add(111,[111],'unproved claim',[66],'623–625','uniquement déterminées','On finite relative polyhedra the first five axioms uniquely determine the two Euler functions and the cell formula.','Cell induction determines compact values; a compact core determines ordinary chi, and closed/open subtraction determines compact supports. Infinite polyhedra are not covered by this argument.')
add(113,[113,114],'self-flagged step',[66],'627','caractérisés par a) à e) sur tout E_infinity','The author asks axiomatic uniqueness on the full conical-at-infinity category. A modern sufficient route uses finite CW homotopy types of the compactification and its core.','The source main sentence says existence, not uniqueness; the question is real. The ANR-based completion is the earlier run’s conditional argument, not a manuscript proof.')
add(115,[115],'idea of the earlier run',[56,58,66],'202;318;625','k corps','In the finite-cell range Euler numbers are independent of the coefficient field, even though Betti numbers and membership in broader finiteness classes can vary.','The alternating cell count is integral. This is a consequence drawn by the earlier run, not an explicit statement on scan56.')
add(116,[116],'idea of the earlier run',[62],'497–500','espaces loc. côniques','A finite simplicial homotopy model has a finite face-poset model via barycentric subdivision.','The inference is conditional on the finite-CW/finite-polyhedral model. It proves no minimization bound in the unrelated FINMON project.')
add(117,[117],'other',[68],'652–666','rel. entre pi_1 et th. de Galois; Conj. de Weil','The theme list names orientation, embeddings, homology, coverings, complex functions and arithmetic topics.','It is programmatic juxtaposition, with no written construction joining Euler axioms, Cartier or projective differential invariants.')
add(120,[120],'idea of the earlier run',[61],'421–425','275;79;354','All six administrative budget column totals agree with the two budget rows; 1976 is 275+79=354.','Exact arithmetic verifies the inserted middle digit’s numerical consistency. It authenticates neither the hand nor a date for the mathematics.')
add(121,[121],'reading question',[69,70],'668–711','Thévenet|Théorème / Contre-Conière|Contre-Courant','The cover’s doubtful heading and name/address layer do not identify a mathematical title or authorial page total.','The transcript supplies names and an address, but no defensible mathematical identification; no personal-identity inference is made.')
add(122,[122,123],'self-flagged step',[83],'1607–1626','k_1 in H3(pi_1(X),pi_2(X)) ?=0','For a connected loop space the source sketches the first Postnikov class and possible cohomology-suspension lifts. It is unclear whether the question-marked zero modifies the class or its ambient group.','The diagram supplies neither a proof nor a decisive grammar. Group vanishing and class vanishing must be assessed separately.')
add(124,[124],'idea of the earlier run',[83],'1610','H3(pi_1(X),pi_2(X))','The ambient cohomology group need not vanish: X=K(C2,1) times K(C2,2) is an infinite loop space with H3(C2;C2)=C2 but zero first class.','The finite normalized-cochain calculation is redone. This refutes the group reading even with arbitrarily many deloopings, while leaving the class reading distinct.')
add(125,[125,128],'unproved claim',[83],'1611–1626','exists k prime_1 in H4(K(pi_1(X),2),pi_2(X))','A connected loop CW-type has its first Postnikov class in the image of one cohomology suspension. Further rows require further compatible deloopings.','The source diagrams suggest exactly this mechanism. One delooping alone cannot manufacture all higher lifts.')
add(126,[126],'unpursued implication',[83],'1613–1615','H5(K(pi_1(X),3),pi_2(X))','The first Postnikov invariant of a connected double loop space vanishes.','This is the later theorem matching two lifts in the diagram; the manuscript only assumes one loop space in its top line. The stronger hypothesis must be stated.')
add(127,[127],'idea of the earlier run',[83],'1607–1610','X=Omega Y','A reduced quadratic module with M=Z, L=Z plus C4, boundary(m,b)=2m and w(x,y)=(0,xy mod4) yields a connected single-loop 2-type with nonzero first class.','The induced action changes b by 2mx; its normalized C2 cocycle has value 2 at (1,1,1), a nonzero class in H3(C2;C4). Algebra is rechecked; realization depends on Baues–Conduché.')
add(129,[129],'idea of the earlier run',[83],'1610–1615','k_1','Even a split double-loop 2-type need not admit a splitting natural in the delooped space.','This is a later functoriality caution supported by Baues–Conduché Theorem1.9, not a question visibly written on the leaf.')
add(130,[130,131,132,133,134],'unproved claim',[62],'483–491','1-complexes topologiques; espaces loc. triangulables','Under the compact recursive-cone interpretation: zero-dimensional links are finite; one-dimensional charts are finite stars; cones on compact finite graphs give local two-dimensional triangulations. Compact dimension-two triangulation and the resulting higher local claims require additional global link results.','The marginal list has several logically different steps. Coning finite graphs verifies the first local implications; Moise for manifolds does not prove all singular-link assertions. The four-dimensional global counterexample is not a refutation of local manifold charts.')
add(135,[135],'reading question',[58],'304','comme ... compacts, par b)','The compact comparison in the provisional proof refers to axiom b even though a is the compactness axiom.','The source axioms show that the cited step uses a. This is a reference-label discrepancy, independent of the later missing support signs.')

# Analytic/integral Schwarzian sequence.
add(136,[136,138,139,140],'unproved claim',[72,73],'716–772;834–840','Il existe un morphisme; G(f,g)+G(g,h)=G(f,h)','For a smooth complex analytic curve, G(f,g)=-2(S(g)-S(f))dt² on étale coordinates is a quadratic-differential cocycle, invariant under constant Möbius transformations, with equality rigidity after a matched 2-jet on a connected domain.','The formula on scan73 supplies existence. The chain rule gives coordinate covariance and constant homographies give zero Schwarzian; the analytic ODE gives rigidity. These are distinct from classification of every admissible operator.')
add(137,[137],'self-flagged step',[72],'747–754','On doit pouvoir montrer que','A reduced universal rational jet expression with poles allowed only where first derivatives vanish should have denominator a monomial in those first derivatives.','The localized polynomial-ring statement follows from unique factorization once arbitrary jets are realized. Passing from the manuscript’s analytic condition and variable coefficients to that universal hypothesis is an explicit missing step.')
add(141,[141,146,147,148],'unproved claim',[72,73],'774–777;802–811;819–831','G prime=phi G; il suffit de supposer d)','The text classifies all admissible rational differential cocycles up to a nowhere-zero scalar on a fixed curve, up to a universal complex scalar under étale naturality, and claims local surjectivity is redundant.','The notes correctly separate this classification from verifying the displayed Schwarzian example. Naturality makes an already existing scalar constant; it does not supply the classification or redundant-axiom proof.')
add(143,[143],'reading question',[72,73],'788;852','c(f)-c(g); c(g)-c(f)','The margin and main torsor statement use opposite difference conventions.','Either convention works after reversing the additive action. Mixing them reverses signs, so neither reading is silently normalized away.')
add(149,[149],'unpursued implication',[73],'836–839','-2 g triple-prime/g prime+3(g double-prime/g prime)^2','The displayed analytic invariant is minus twice the standard Schwarzian derivative difference.','Direct substitution verifies the normalization and fixes the sign for the second-order ODE comparison.')
add(150,[142,144,145,150],'unproved claim',[72,73],'779–800;842–855','ce dernier morphisme est un iso','On a smooth complex analytic curve, projective étale coordinates modulo locally constant PGL2 form a torsor under quadratic differentials, and the displayed quotient morphism is a sheaf isomorphism.','Projective charts glue by Möbius invariance; matching 2-jets gives local injectivity and ratios of independent second-order-ODE solutions give local surjectivity. No canonical global section or globally single-valued solution follows.')
add(152,[152,154],'unproved claim',[73],'888–897','les coefficients doivent être dans Z; unicité au signe près','The source proposes an integral natural-operator classification with universal integral coefficients and uniqueness up to a unit of Z.','Units of Z are plus/minus one only after the general classification is established. Formula checks cannot prove the classification of all operators.')
add(153,[151,153,155,156,157,159,160],'unproved claim',[73],'883–928','D_t^(n); G0; 1/12 G','For an étale coordinate over any commutative base, set Q(f)=-D3f/D1f+(D2f/D1f)^2. Then (Q(g)-Q(f))dt² is the integral cocycle G0, and ordinary G=12G0. It is nonzero in characteristics2/3 despite vanishing of ordinary G; the t²/t³ tests require invertible first jets.','Universal Hasse-jet composition proves the cocycle over Z before reduction. G0(t,t+t²)=1 in characteristic2 and G0(t,t+t³)=-1 in characteristic3. The valid cube value is 2/(3t²); it vanishes in characteristic2. Division by12 is notation for the independent integral formula.')
add(158,[158],'reading question',[73],'913–915','G0(t,t²)=1/(2t) dt²','The square-coordinate test should evaluate to dt²/(4t²) wherever 2t is a unit.','Hasse derivatives are D1(t²)=2t,D2(t²)=1,D3(t²)=0, so the formula gives 1/(4t²), disagreeing with the retained printed value. This is mathematical evidence only.')

